        "title": "Mass Incarceration as Policy Choice",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Systems-dynamics bridge:** Mass incarceration exhibits a textbook positive feedback loop — a self-reinforcing cycle that resists equilibrium correction. The loop: arrest → criminal record → employment exclusion → income loss → recidivism pressure → re-arrest → longer record → deeper exclusion → higher recidivism. Each iteration through the loop strengthens the signal (more convictions, longer records, narrower legal options), which is why incarceration rates rose continuously for 35 years even as crime rates fell after 1994. In control-system terms, the system lacks a stabilizing negative feedback mechanism: the self-regulating element (parole offsetting sentence length) was removed by truth-in-sentencing laws in the 1980s-1990s, and the growth signal (mandatory minimums, three-strikes) dominated without a counteracting governor. The stock-and-flow structure matters: even if intake slows dramatically, the stock (incarcerated population) declines only as slowly as sentences expire — prison populations are sticky in the direction of growth. This explains why reform requires both reducing new admissions *and* reducing time served for the existing population."
    },
