        "title": "Mass Incarceration as Policy Choice",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The United States incarcerates at a rate 5-10x higher than comparable democratic nations. This is not a natural response to crime -- it is the result of specific policy decisions made from the 1970s onward. Understanding mass incarceration requires separating the causes (policy choices), the mechanisms (drug war + mandatory minimums + sentencing guidelines), and the consequences (individual, family, community, fiscal)."
